      "lede": "On August 11, Judge Vince Chhabria denied Meta's motion to quash subpoenas in four coordinated cases — Nos. 3:25-cv-09579, 3:26-cv-02333, 3:26-cv-03725, and 3:26-cv-04053 — ruling, without a written opinion, that the subpoenas were justified for the reasons stated in the plaintiffs' opposition briefs. The order, docketed in Kadrey v. Meta (N.D. Cal.",
      "body_md": "On August 11, Judge Vince Chhabria denied Meta's motion to quash subpoenas in four coordinated cases — Nos. 3:25-cv-09579, 3:26-cv-02333, 3:26-cv-03725, and 3:26-cv-04053 — ruling, without a written opinion, that the subpoenas were justified for the reasons stated in the plaintiffs' opposition briefs. The order, docketed in *Kadrey v. Meta* (N.D. Cal. No. 3:23-cv-03417) at Dkt. 807, keeps third-party discovery from multiple related proceedings on track. Judge Chhabria had already, on August 10, granted Meta's own motion for leave to serve discovery on the plaintiffs in the *Kadrey* case itself (Dkt. 806), so both sides are now simultaneously pressed into active written discovery. On August 12, the parties filed a Joint Case Management Statement (Dkt. 808), signaling that the August 14 case-management conference — now set by Clerk's Notice as a Zoom teleconference — will address the coordination of those parallel discovery obligations. The back-to-back quash denial and the joint CMC statement together indicate that pre-trial discovery in *Kadrey* and its cluster is moving on a compressed front, with the scope of permissible third-party discovery now settled in plaintiffs' favor for purposes of the coordinated subpoenas.\n\nIn *Britannica v. OpenAI* (S.D.N.Y. No. 1:26-cv-02097, Judge Sidney H. Stein), the docket recorded a Memo Endorsement at Dkt. 59 on August 12, one week after Judge Stein entered two orders setting deadlines (Dkts. 57–58, August 7) following the parties' proposed stipulation at Dkt. 56. The content of Dkt. 59 is not publicly available in secondary sources; the filing type — a memo endorsement — is a chambers signoff on a letter or stipulation and is procedural on its face, though its importance depends on what it endorsed. \nThe case sits in the Southern District of New York\n and is also an associated case in the OpenAI MDL, \nwith a status conference before Magistrate Judge Ona T. Wang having been held on March 10, 2026, in the MDL context.\n Until the endorsed document is retrieved, the August 12 entry is classified as procedural-but-potentially-important and held without further characterization.\n\nIn *Getty Images (US) v. Stability AI* (N.D. Cal. No. 3:25-cv-06891, Judge Trina L. Thompson), a substantive opposition brief landed at Dkt. 71 on August 12, opposing whichever pending motion drew it — the prior day's Dkt. 70, an Administrative Motion under Civil Local Rule 7-11, itself followed a pattern already established with a parallel Administrative Motion at Dkt. 68 on August 6 and an opposition at Dkt. 69 on August 10. Civil Local Rule 7-11 governs administrative motions for routine relief (seal, file under seal, extend page limits, or request a stipulation), so the pair of administrative motions with oppositions signals a collateral dispute about discovery or briefing mechanics rather than a merits filing; both the Dkt. 68/69 and Dkt. 70/71 pairs are substantive only if the underlying Administrative Motion carries a merits dimension, which the docket labels alone do not confirm. \nGetty refiled in the Northern District of California on August 14, 2025, after voluntarily dismissing the Delaware action, advancing direct copyright, DMCA §1202, and Lanham Act trademark claims\n — all of which remain at the pleadings stage given the amended answer filed at Dkt. 67 on July 2. The next confirmed scheduling marker is the Further Case Management Conference set for November 5, 2026, with a Joint CMC Statement due October 29, 2026.\n\n*Hachette v. Google (Gemini)* (S.D.N.Y. No. 1:26-cv-05870, Judge Loretta A. Preska) and *Hachette v. Meta (Llama)* (S.D.N.Y. No. 1:26-cv-03689, Judge P. Kevin Castel) recorded no new substantive docket activity. In *Hachette v. Meta*, the next concrete milestone is the conference before Judge Castel on September 15, 2026, at 3:00 p.m. in Courtroom 11D, where Google's transfer motion and related pre-motion practice in the *Hachette v. Google* matter remain in briefing following the reply filed at Dkt. 26 on August 10. *Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence* (3d Cir. No. 25-2153) remains under submission following oral argument before a panel of Judges Restrepo, Montgomery-Reeves, and Bove on June 11, 2026; no decision has issued.\n\nThe next concrete milestone across the group is the *Kadrey* Zoom teleconference before Judge Chhabria, scheduled for August 14, 2026, at which discovery scope and coordination across the cluster are expected to be addressed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Getty Images v. Stability AI: Case Status May 2026](https://ailawsuittracker.com/cases/getty-v-stability-ai/) — AI Lawsuit Tracker, May 25, 2026\n- [Encyclopaedia Britannica, Inc. v. OpenAI, Inc., 1:26-cv-02097 — CourtListener](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/72492986/encyclopaedia-britannica-inc-v-openai-inc/) — CourtListener / RECAP, July 14, 2026\n- [Britannica, Merriam-Webster Accuse OpenAI of Copying Their Works](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/ip-law/britannica-merriam-webster-accuse-openai-of-copying-their-works) — Bloomberg Law, March 16, 2026\n- [Getty Images (US), Inc. v. Stability AI, Ltd., 3:25-cv-06891 — CourtListener](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/71112094/getty-images-us-inc-v-stability-ai-ltd/) — CourtListener / RECAP, June 24, 2026\n- [Kadrey v. Meta Platforms, Inc., 3:23-cv-03417 — CourtListener](https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/67569326/kadrey-v-meta-platforms-inc/) — CourtListener / RECAP\n\n---\n\n*This briefing is provided for informational purposes by Turnpage Digital Markets and does not constitute legal advice.*",
