      "lede": "On August 12, McDermott Will & Schulte LLP filed a Notice of Withdrawal of Appearance (Dkt. 73), exiting the case entirely on behalf of TRAG LLC and RG Energy LLC — the first-lien creditors whose $87.3 million lien position defines the secured layer of this capital structure. The withdrawal is the substantive event of the past 24 hours and warrants attention.",
      "body_md": "On August 12, McDermott Will & Schulte LLP filed a Notice of Withdrawal of Appearance (Dkt. 73), exiting the case entirely on behalf of TRAG LLC and RG Energy LLC — the first-lien creditors whose $87.3 million lien position defines the secured layer of this capital structure. The withdrawal is the substantive event of the past 24 hours and warrants attention. As recently as August 7, McDermott Will & Schulte had admitted two partners pro hac vice (Dkts. 69-72), making the exit unusually abrupt: two admissions and a notice of appearance, all reversed within five days. No substitute counsel has appeared on behalf of TRAG or RG Energy as of this writing. \nTRAG LLC and RG Energy LLC, both connected to investor Tony Robbins, hold liens and security interests granted at the end of June in the amount of $87.3 million.\n That position gives them leverage over essentially every outcome in this case — plan confirmation, any § 363 sale process, and cash-collateral use — making their counsel posture directly relevant to recovery prospects for the broader creditor body.\n\nThe practical consequences of an unrepresented first-lien creditor are immediate. Without active counsel of record, TRAG and RG Energy cannot file an objection, respond to a motion, or participate at the September 3 final hearing on the first-day critical operations orders — wages, utilities, and critical vendor payments — without first securing replacement representation and moving that new counsel through admission. The objection deadline for those final orders is August 19, six days out. If no new counsel appears before then, the entities most capable of contesting the final first-day relief lose their standing to do so. Whether the withdrawal reflects a strategic decision by TRAG/RG Energy to allow the operational orders to pass uncontested, a breakdown in the client-counsel relationship, or a transition to different outside counsel not yet announced is unknown on the current record. None of those readings is verifiable from the docket alone, and reasonable observers could disagree on which is most plausible. Dkts. 74, 75, and 76 — the U.S. Trustee's scheduling request, Zoom logistics notice, and formal Notice of Meeting of Creditors — set the § 341(a) meeting for September 2, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. ET, with a § 523 dischargeability objection deadline of November 2, 2026. Those filings are administrative infrastructure; they are noted for timing but carry no independent bearing on creditor recovery.\n\nThe next hard deadline is August 19 — objections to the final orders on wages, utilities, and taxes are due at 4:00 p.m. ET per Dkt. 49. The absence of counsel for TRAG and RG Energy against that deadline is the position to watch. The September 2 § 341(a) meeting follows, and the September 3 final hearings on the first-day operational orders are the next substantive event on the calendar.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Future of former Pleasants Power Plant uncertain as Omnis Pleasants files for bankruptcy](https://www.newsandsentinel.com/news/business/2026/07/future-of-former-pleasants-power-plant-uncertain-as-omnis-pleasants-files-for-bankruptcy/) — News and Sentinel, July 2026\n- [Pleasants Power Station's Omnis firm reports 600+ creditors](https://www.herald-dispatch.com/news/omnis-pleasants-reports-600-creditors-70-in-wv-amid-uncertain-coal-plant-future/article_5b21adc9-1c75-433a-be51-5ed26f74a819.html) — Herald-Dispatch, August 5, 2026\n- [Pleasants Power Station's Omnis firm reports 600+ creditors](https://www.wvgazettemail.com/news/legal_affairs/omnis-pleasants-reports-600-creditors-70-in-wv-amid-uncertain-coal-plant-future/article_1f5045c2-ed17-4993-b2e5-8c7ede971f62.html) — Charleston Gazette-Mail, August 4, 2026\n- [Power Struggle: New Pleasants Power Leadership Fights With Former Management In Bankruptcy Filings](https://www.theintelligencer.net/news/top-headlines/2026/08/power-struggle-new-pleasants-power-leadership-fights-with-former-management-in-bankruptcy-filings/) — The Intelligencer, August 2026\n- [McDermott Will & Schulte — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McDermott_Will_%26_Schulte) — Wikipedia\n\n*This briefing is provided for informational purposes by Turnpage Digital Markets and does not constitute legal advice.*",
