      "lede": "On August 11, Firy filed two additional deposition notices (Dkts. 61–62) and two affidavits (Dkts.",
      "body_md": "On August 11, Firy filed two additional deposition notices (Dkts. 61–62) and two affidavits (Dkts. 66–67) in Case No. 26-11217 before Judge Walrath, both filings bearing on the contested Chapter 15 recognition proceeding. The deposition notices extend Firy's witness program beyond the three already noticed August 8 — Oriel Bachar, Pinni Yaniv, and James Kong (Dkts. 53–55) — adding two further deponents whose identities are not specified in the docket entry text. That expansion signals Firy is pressing a broad factual record ahead of the recognition hearing, consistent with the strategic posture of a creditor seeking to challenge the foreign proceeding's eligibility or the scope of the temporary injunction Judge Walrath granted August 9.\n\nThe two affidavits (Dkts. 66–67) are the more consequential August 11 filings. In a contested Chapter 15 recognition proceeding, sworn affidavits typically function as evidentiary support for a party's position on recognition eligibility, the debtor's center-of-main-interests, or the terms under which a temporary injunction should be continued or dissolved. The docket record does not specify which party submitted them or what propositions they address; that detail will control how they bear on Firy's recovery posture. If they are Papaya-side submissions, they likely shore up the COMI analysis or the Israeli fund structure. If Firy-side, they are likely directed at contesting one of those same points. Until the documents themselves are reviewed, the filing cannot be assigned a directional weight — but their timing, three affidavits-and-depositions days into a contested proceeding with no recognition hearing date yet set, confirms both sides are building a litigation record at pace.\n\nThree further pro hac vice motions were filed August 10 (Dkts. 58–60), continuing the counsel-buildout on the Firy side that the Latham & Watkins admissions (Dkts. 56–57, approving Ardeljan and Quartarolo) completed the same day. Dkts. 63–65, three additional pro hac vice motions filed August 11, deepen that roster further. No recognition hearing date appears on the docket as of this briefing; that date, once set, will be the controlling deadline against which both sides' deposition and affidavit schedules are paced.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Papaya Gaming wins U.S. bankruptcy protection after $719 million ruling](https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/idop8ipjs) — Calcalist / CTech, August 9, 2026\n- [Papaya Gaming wins temporary stay after $719 million US judgment](https://www.jpost.com/israel-news/article-904473) — The Jerusalem Post, August 2026\n- [Why a popular gaming company filed Chapter 15 bankruptcy](https://www.thestreet.com/retail/gambling-company-papaya-gaming-files-bankruptcy-after-719-million-verdict) — TheStreet, August 5, 2026\n\n*This briefing is provided for informational purposes by Turnpage Digital Markets and does not constitute legal advice.*",
